      <p class="small muted aside">
        COMPASS grew out of the method developed in my <a href="/publications">honors thesis</a>, which also evaluated MRI
        and microscopy data. The paper is currently under review.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">In progress</p>
      <h2>A landscape framework for memorization</h2>
      <p>
        COMPASS treats a model's response to noise as a signal. This project asks what that signal is a measurement
        of. My thesis began to describe memorized samples as sitting in unusually stable regions of a model's response
        landscape, regions that resist small perturbations. I am now working to make that picture precise, using the dynamics of how outputs change under perturbation to map the landscape around training and unseen data.
      </p>
      <p>
        The goal is a mechanistic account of membership inference: why the signal appears at low noise, why its
        direction flips between models, and whether memorization leaves measurable geometric signatures in a model's representations. I aim to incorporate tools from information geometry and statistical physics to describe these state spaces, developing methods that may reveal previously underexplored relationships between memorization, utility and security. An explanation of that kind would turn privacy attacks from empirical tools into instruments
        for studying how large models store what they learn.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">In progress</p>
      <h2>Scaling laws for membership inference</h2>
      <p>
        Privacy audits are expensive, and the models that most need them are often too large to audit directly. In
        physics, scaling laws let us predict a system's behavior far beyond the regime we can measure, and in machine
        learning they already predict how loss falls with model size, data and compute. This project asks whether
        privacy risk follows similar laws.
      </p>
      <p>
        I am exploring empirical scaling laws for membership inference attacks: how attack success changes with the
        resources available to an attacker and with the scale of the model and its training data. If those trends are
        predictable, a defender could run small, low-cost audits and extrapolate to estimate what a well-resourced
        attacker could achieve, making privacy risk measurable for models too large to test directly.
      </p>
    </section>
